==Controversies==
{{Main|Criticism of the BBC|BBC controversies}}

[[File:What would Orwell have thought of the BBC's Pro-Israel propaganda (53335024694).jpg|thumb|[[Gaza war protests in the United Kingdom|Pro-Palestinian protester]] accusing the BBC of pro-Israel bias during the [[Gaza war]]]]
Throughout its existence the BBC has faced numerous accusations regarding many topics: the [[2003 invasion of Iraq|Iraq war]], the [[Gaza war]], [[politics]], [[ethics]] and [[religion]], as well as funding and staffing. It also has been involved in numerous controversies because of its coverage of specific news stories and programming. The BBC has been accused repeatedly of anti-Israel and anti-Palestinian bias.<ref>{{Cite web |title=The BBC Says It's "Impartial." But Its Own Staff Say Otherwise {{!}} Al Jazeera Media Institute |url=https://institute.aljazeera.net/en/ajr/article/3250 |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=institute.aljazeera.net |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Analysis of BBC News headlines shows scale of anti-Israel bias |url=https://www.camera.org/article/analysis-of-bbc-news-headlines-shows-scale-of-anti-israel-bias/ |access-date=2026-07-26 |website=CAMERA |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Claims of liberal and left-wing bias===
{{see also|2025 BBC editorial bias allegations}}
The BBC has long faced accusations of liberal and left-wing bias.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2012/oct/10/bbc-review-liberal-bias |title=BBC reporting scrutinised after accusations of liberal bias |newspaper=The Guardian |first=Josh |last=Halliday |date=10 October 2012 |quote=The corporation has long faced accusations of liberal and leftwing bias from politicians and other sections of the media. |access-date=6 August 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170807023223/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2012/oct/10/bbc-review-liberal-bias |archive-date=7 August 2017 |url-status=live}}</ref> Accusations of a bias against the [[Premiership of Margaret Thatcher]] and the [[Conservative Party (UK)|Conservative Party]] were often made against the BBC by members of that government, with [[Margaret Thatcher]] herself considering the broadcaster's news coverage to be biased and irresponsible.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/politics/margaret-thatcher/11313380/Margaret-Thatcher-conducted-covert-war-against-BBC.html |title=Margaret Thatcher conducted covert war against BBC |work=Telegraph Online |date=30 December 2014 |access-date=9 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180411082401/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/politics/margaret-thatcher/11313380/Margaret-Thatcher-conducted-covert-war-against-BBC.html |archive-date=11 April 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2011, [[Peter Sissons]], a main news presenter at the BBC from 1989 to 2009, said that "at the core of the BBC, in its very [[DNA]], is a way of thinking that is firmly of the Left".<ref>{{cite book |last=Sissons |first=Peter |title=When One Door Closes |publisher=Bite Back |year=2011}}</ref>

Another BBC presenter, [[Andrew Marr]], commented that "the BBC is not impartial or neutral. It has a liberal bias, not so much a party-political bias. It is better expressed as a cultural liberal bias." Former BBC director [[Roger Mosey]] classified it as "liberal defensive".<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/comment/personal-view/3633619/The-BBCs-commitment-to-bias-is-no-laughing-matter.html |location=London |newspaper=The Daily Telegraph |first=Tom |last=Leonard |title=The BBC's commitment to bias is no laughing matter |date=27 October 2006 |access-date=5 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180411115901/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/comment/personal-view/3633619/The-BBCs-commitment-to-bias-is-no-laughing-matter.html |archive-date=11 April 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/6764779.stm |work=BBC News |first=Torin |last=Douglas |title=Does the BBC have a bias problem? |date=18 June 2007 |access-date=19 June 2015 |archive-date=19 March 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220319060745/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/6764779.stm |url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/bbc/11690479/BBC-accused-of-liberal-bias-by-former-director.html |title=BBC accused of liberal bias by former director |newspaper=Telegraph |first=Victoria |last=Ward |date=22 June 2015 |access-date=5 April 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180411214631/https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/bbc/11690479/BBC-accused-of-liberal-bias-by-former-director.html |archive-date=11 April 2018 |url-status=live}}</ref> In 2022, the BBC chairman, [[Richard Sharp (banker)|Richard Sharp]], said that the BBC "does have a liberal bias" and that the institution is "fighting against it".<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2022/dec/03/bbc-chairman-criticises-emily-maitlis-newsnight-comments-on-dominic-cummings |title=BBC chairman criticises Emily Maitlis' Newsnight comments on Dominic Cummings |first=Nadeem |last=Badshah |date=3 December 2022 |work=The Guardian |location=London |access-date=4 December 2022 |archive-date=3 December 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221203233724/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2022/dec/03/bbc-chairman-criticises-emily-maitlis-newsnight-comments-on-dominic-cummings |url-status=live}}</ref> In November 2025, [[2025 BBC editorial bias allegations|allegations]] by ''[[The Daily Telegraph]]'' that the BBC had deceptively spliced a [[Donald Trump]] speech preceding the [[January 6 United States Capitol attack]] in a documentary led to several BBC executives resigning, including the head of the BBC.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 November 2025 |title=Top BBC bosses resign after criticism of the broadcaster's editing of a Trump speech |url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/wireStory/bbc-director-resigns-after-criticism-broadcasters-editing-trump-127357626 |access-date=11 November 2025 |website=ABC News}}</ref>

===Claims of right-wing bias===
Writing for ''[[The Guardian]]'' in 2018, the left-wing columnist [[Owen Jones]] stated "the truth is the BBC is stacked full of rightwingers,"<ref>{{Cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2014/mar/17/bbc-leftwing-bias-non-existent-myth |title=It's the BBC's rightwing bias that is the threat to democracy and journalism |first=Owen |last=Jones |date=17 March 2014 |work=The Guardian |location=London |access-date=11 December 2016 |archive-date=21 December 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161221070448/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2014/mar/17/bbc-leftwing-bias-non-existent-myth |url-status=live}}</ref> and cited as an example of bias its employment of "ultra-[[Thatcherite]]" [[Andrew Neil]] as a politics presenter.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2018/apr/11/bbc-andrew-neil-media-politics |title=If the BBC is politically neutral, how does it explain Andrew Neil? |newspaper=The Guardian |location=London |first=Owen |last=Jones |date=11 April 2018 |access-date=7 December 2018 |archive-date=9 December 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181209123856/https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2018/apr/11/bbc-andrew-neil-media-politics |url-status=live}}</ref> In August 2022, former BBC presenter [[Emily Maitlis]] stated that BBC board member [[Robbie Gibb]], who had worked as an editorial advisor to the right-wing channel [[GB News]], and former Prime Minister [[Boris Johnson]], who had appointed him to the board, was an "active agent of the Conservative party" who played a significant role in determining the nature of the corporation's news output.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Waterson |first1=Jim |author-link=Jim Waterson |title=Emily Maitlis says "active Tory party agent" shaping BBC news output |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2022/aug/24/emily-maitlis-says-active-tory-party-agent-shaping-bbc-news-output |access-date=11 November 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=24 August 2022 |archive-date=24 August 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220824180513/https://www.theguardian.com/media/2022/aug/24/emily-maitlis-says-active-tory-party-agent-shaping-bbc-news-output |url-status=live}}</ref> In November 2025, [[LibDem]] leader [[Ed Davey]] called for Gibb's removal from the BBC board because of Gibb's alleged role in the 2025 BBC editorial bias controversy.<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 November 2025 |title=Ed Davey calls for Robbie Gibb to be ousted from BBC board |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/uk/politics/bbc-robbie-gibb-ed-davey-tim-davie-b2862736.html |access-date=11 November 2025 |website=The Independent |language=en}}</ref>

A 2018 opinion poll by BMG Research found that 40% of the British public thought that the BBC was politically partisan, with a nearly even split between those who believed it leaned to the left or right.<ref name="economist-20190109">{{cite news |url=https://www.economist.com/graphic-detail/2019/01/09/question-time-the-bbcs-flagship-political-show-gets-a-female-host |title=Question Time, the BBC's flagship political show, gets a female host |newspaper=The Economist |location=London |date=9 January 2019 |access-date=16 January 2019 |archive-date=16 January 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190116004014/https://www.economist.com/graphic-detail/2019/01/09/question-time-the-bbcs-flagship-political-show-gets-a-female-host |url-status=live}}</ref>

=== Accusations of bias on transgender topics ===
The BBC has been accused by politicians, media sources, BBC staff, and trans rights advocates of creating a "moral panic" against trans rights and of wider transphobia in reporting,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dawson |first=Bethany |title=The BBC and The Times are accused of stoking a 'moral panic' against the trans community |url=https://www.businessinsider.com/the-bbc-and-the-times-accused-of-moral-panic-against-trans-community-2021-11 |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=Business Insider |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=John |first=Tara |date=9 October 2021 |title=Analysis: Anti-trans rhetoric is rife in the British media. Little is being done to extinguish the flames |url=https://www.cnn.com/2021/10/09/uk/uk-trans-rights-gender-critical-media-intl-gbr-cmd |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=CNN |language=en}}</ref> citing examples such as the article [["We're being pressured into sex by some trans women"]], in which the BBC cited a social media poll from an anti-trans group to say that transgender women were pressuring lesbians to have sex with them under the threat of being labelled transphobic.<ref name="Hansford">{{Cite web |last=Hansford |first=Amelia |date=10 November 2025 |title=Right-wingers claim the BBC is 'pro-trans' – that couldn't be further from the truth |url=https://www.thepinknews.com/2025/11/10/bbc-pro-trans-nope-not-even-close/ |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=PinkNews |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kanter |first=Jake Media |date=5 November 2021 |title=Young BBC staffers angered by trans story claiming harassment of lesbians |url=https://www.thetimes.com/culture/tv-radio/article/young-bbc-staffers-angered-by-trans-story-claiming-harassment-of-lesbians-mwndtxf7s |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=The Times |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=28 October 2021 |title=Lesbians stand by trans women in open letter after 'dangerous' BBC article |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/nbc-out/out-news/lesbians-stand-trans-women-open-letter-dangerous-bbc-article-rcna3903 |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref> Additionally oft-cited are its policy of "balancing" appearances by transgender people with activists from gender-critical groups, its manual of style which requires any allegations of transphobia to be challenged by the news presenter, and its policy against using the term "cisgender".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Duffy |first=Nick |date=15 December 2020 |title=Ofcom boss declares it 'extremely inappropriate' for BBC to 'balance' trans people with anti-trans activists |url=https://www.thepinknews.com/2020/12/15/ofcom-melanie-dawes-bbc-lgb-alliance-john-nicolson-trans-transphobia-balance/ |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=PinkNews |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kanter |first=Jake |date=22 March 2024 |title=BBC Presenters Told To Challenge Guests Who Label Others "Transphobic" After J.K. Rowling Errors |url=https://deadline.com/2024/03/bbc-sex-and-gender-briefing-jk-rowling-1235864310/ |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=Deadline |language=en-US}}</ref>

The BBC has also been accused by [[Gender-critical feminism|gender-critical]] groups and BBC staff of having a pro-trans bias, primarily for "failing to make it clear that transgender women are biological males".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Access Restricted |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/11/05/bbc-trans-coverage-censored-own-reporters/ |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=www.telegraph.co.uk}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Access Restricted |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2025/11/11/bbc-ignored-pro-trans-bias-in-sports-stories/ |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=www.telegraph.co.uk}}</ref> Such accusations have alleged that the BBC's LGBT desk has been "captured by a small group of people" intent on promoting a pro-trans agenda.<ref name="Hansford"/><ref>{{Cite web |author=Sanchez Manning |date=10 November 2025 |title='I was forced out of BBC News over gender-critical views' |url=https://www.thetimes.com/uk/media/article/bbc-news-editors-trans-issues-junior-staff-bxjp3t7j3 |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=www.thetimes.com |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 November 2025 |title=When will the BBC face up to the truth? |url=https://sex-matters.org/posts/updates/when-will-the-bbc-face-up-to-the-truth/ |access-date=13 November 2025 |website=Sex Matters |language=en-GB}}</ref> Shortly thereafter, the BBC began running articles referring to trans women as "biological males who identify as women".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Perry |first=Sophie |date=28 November 2025 |title=BBC denies telling staff to describe trans women as 'biological males' |url=https://www.thepinknews.com/2025/11/28/bbc-trans-biological-males/ |access-date=11 December 2025 |website=PinkNews |language=en-US}}</ref>

